%I A204326 #20 Sep 05 2026 21:51:37
%S A204326 0,0,1,4,17,80,496,4022,41152,509786,7315315,117910956,2094235237,
%T A204326 40494846422,845456761212,18941262210342,453087074364105,
%U A204326 11523352997520850,310466719672791282,8832728879103824899
%N A204326 Number of connected cubic graphs on 2n nodes with chromatic number 3.
%C A204326 The number of cubic graphs on 2n nodes with chromatic number 1 begins 0, 0, 1, 1, 2, 5, 13, ... . If we had more terms this would warrant its own entry.
%C A204326 The sequence requested above is A006823. By Brooks' theorem, all connected cubic graphs have chromatic number 2 or 3, except for the complete graph K4, hence A006823(n) + a(n) = A002851(n) for n >= 3. - _Martin Fuller_, Sep 04 2026
%H A204326 F. C. Bussemaker, S. Cobeljic, L. M. Cvetkovic and J. J. Seidel, <a href="http://alexandria.tue.nl/repository/books/252909.pdf">Computer investigations of cubic graphs</a>, T.H.-Report 76-WSK-01, Technological University Eindhoven, Dept. Mathematics, 1976.
%H A204326 Brendan D. McKay and Gordon F. Royle, <a href="https://users.cecs.anu.edu.au/~bdm/papers/Gobstoppers.pdf">Constructing the cubic graphs on up to 20 vertices</a>, Ars Combinatoria, 21A (1986) 129-140. See p. 137.
%F A204326 a(1) = a(2) = 0. For n >= 3: a(n) = A002851(n) - A006823(n). - _Martin Fuller_, Sep 04 2026
%o A204326 (nauty) for n in {4..22..2}; do geng -qc -d3 -D3 "${n}" | countg -q1 -N3 -~b; done # _Freddy Barrera_, Aug 21 2026
%Y A204326 Cf. A002851, A006823.
%K A204326 nonn,more,changed
%O A204326 1,4
%A A204326 _N. J. A. Sloane_, Jan 14 2012
